; PS/2 keyboard + mouse receptacles, on the I/O panel near the top of the back —
; where an AT/ATX tower puts them. Stacked vertically, mouse above keyboard. The
; PC99 colour code is baked into each mesh (purple keyboard, green mouse) and is the
; only thing telling the two apart.
;
; Baked by Tools/gen/gen_ps2.gd from the manufacturer's DETAIL D, so the six sockets and
; the central key slot land where the male's contacts do. Replaced a flat coloured
; ring with a dark disc in it, which had neither.
;
; Ry(180), NOT the Rx(180) every port SEAT on this panel carries. Both point the
; mesh's +Z out of the back, but Rx negates Y as well — and the PS/2 contact field is
; not symmetric about Y (5 and 6 up, 1 and 2 down), so it would come out upside down
; and the keyway would sit at the bottom. Ry negates X instead, and the field IS
; symmetric about X, so the set maps onto itself and up stays up.
;
; z -0.2215: 1.5 mm proud of the case's back face at -0.22. Everything the mesh draws
; is at local z > 0, so nothing is left behind the panel for it to occlude.
[node name="Ps2Keyboard" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.340, -0.2215)
mesh = ExtResource("4_ps2jackkb")

[node name="Ps2Mouse" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.372, -0.2215)
mesh = ExtResource("5_ps2jackms")

; Silkscreen above each socket, the way a real I/O panel names its ports.
;
; The words below are what the EDITOR shows and what survives if the model script
; never runs. At runtime RetroSystemModelPCTower._label_ports overwrites each with a
; Nerd Font icon and re-sizes it — the font has to be chained on in code, because a
; .tscn cannot put the symbol font behind the project default and the codepoints are
; private-use, so authored here they would every one render as tofu.
;
; Turned 180 about Y so the words read the right way round from BEHIND the tower,
; and set 0.5 mm proud of the case's back face at -0.22 so they do not z-fight it.
;
; 5 mm of line, which is what the tightest gap will take: keyboard socket to VGA
; flange is 8.2 mm, leaving 1.6 mm each side. Each label is centred in the gap ABOVE
; its own socket rather than spaced evenly — the ports are not evenly spaced either.
;
; font_size 48 with pixel_size 0.000104, NOT font_size 5 with pixel_size 0.001. Both
; give a 5 mm line, but font_size is the size the glyphs are RASTERISED at: asking
; for 5 builds a five-pixel-tall atlas and then magnifies it, and the words came out
; as smears. Rasterise large and scale down — the recipe vr_hinge.gd uses for its
; hint icons (font_size 96, pixel_size = size / 96).
;
; outline_size 0. Label3D outlines by default, and dark text with a dark outline on
; a beige panel fuses into a blob at this size; the outline exists to lift floating
; billboard text off a busy background, which is the opposite of silkscreen.
[node name="MouseLabel" type="Label3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.3875, -0.2205)
pixel_size = 0.000104
text = "Mouse PS/2"
font_size = 48
outline_size = 0
modulate = Color(0.16, 0.15, 0.12, 1)

[node name="KeyboardLabel" type="Label3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.356, -0.2205)
pixel_size = 0.000104
text = "Keyboard PS/2"
font_size = 48
outline_size = 0
modulate = Color(0.16, 0.15, 0.12, 1)

[node name="VgaLabel" type="Label3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.3284, -0.2205)
pixel_size = 0.000104
text = "VGA"
font_size = 48
outline_size = 0
modulate = Color(0.16, 0.15, 0.12, 1)

[node name="SpeakerLabel" type="Label3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.3055, -0.2205)
pixel_size = 0.000104
text = "Speakers"
font_size = 48
outline_size = 0
modulate = Color(0.16, 0.15, 0.12, 1)

; Where the plugs seat, rotated 180 about X.
;
; NOT identity, which was the first guess and put the plug in backwards — barrel
; buried in the case, connector poking out the back. The snap zone does not seat a
; pickable at the zone's own basis: SnapGrabPoint in controller_cable.tscn is
; itself rotated 180 about X, and that composes in, so a zone at identity yields a
; plug at diag(1,-1,-1). Pre-cancelling it here lands the plug at identity, which
; for a BACK-facing socket drives the connector (+Z on the mesh) into the case and
; trails the cord (-Z) out the back.
;
; Measured, not derived — the derivation is what got it backwards. A front-facing
; socket wants a different correction again; see nes_model.gd's roll.
;
; Port 1 = keyboard, matching the purple socket above it; port 2 = mouse.
[node name="PortSeat1" type="Marker3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.340, -0.2235)

[node name="PortSeat2" type="Marker3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.372, -0.2235)

; Port 3 = the game port, the 15-pin socket a joystick or pad plugged into. On the
; sound card on real hardware, which is why it sits down here with the audio jacks
; rather than up in the PS/2 stack: y 0.262 is 32 mm below LineOut (0.294), so the
; 12.6 mm flange clears that by 25 mm and stays well above the RCA row at 0.130.
;
; Rx(180) like the two seats above, and for the same measured reason.
;
; A pad in this socket drives libretro port 0 whatever cabinet slot it is —
; RetroSystem._libretro_port_for pins it, the way it already pins a computer mouse.
[node name="PortSeat3" type="Marker3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.262, -0.2235)

[node name="GameLabel" type="Label3D" parent="Back"]
transform = Transform3D(-1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.2724, -0.2205)
pixel_size = 0.000104
text = "Game"
font_size = 48
outline_size = 0
modulate = Color(0.16, 0.15, 0.12, 1)

; The video card's socket, on the I/O panel directly under the PS/2 stack — where a
; board with onboard video puts it. y 0.318 is 22 mm below the keyboard socket, so
; the 12.6 mm flange clears that 15 mm barrel by 8 mm; the 30.8 mm flange spans
; x -0.070..-0.040, well inside the 190 mm case.
;
; Authored here rather than built by system.gd::_build_av_ports, which only knows
; how to lay out a row of RCA channels. RcaPort.get_device() walks UP for
; on_av_topology_changed, and this model is a child of the RetroSystem, so a socket
; dropped into the model scene resolves its device with no wiring of its own.
;
; z -0.224, not -0.221: the zone sits where a seated plug's ORIGIN belongs, which
; for this connector is 3 mm proud of the panel — the male hood face stops against
; the female shell's rim. The jack child inside vga_port.tscn carries the matching
; -3 mm so its flange lands on the panel at -0.221. NOT the RCA pair's 10 mm.
;
; Rotated 180 about X for the same measured reason PortSeat1/2 above are: a zone at
; identity yields a plug at diag(1,-1,-1) with its hood buried in the case.
;
; direction = 0 is OUT. `channel` is not set here — VgaPort pins it to VIDEO, since
; VGA has no other channel to carry. The tower keeps its three RCA sockets: sound
; still has to leave on those, exactly as it did on the real machine.
[node name="VgaPort" parent="Back" instance=ExtResource("2_vgaport")]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.318, -0.2285)
direction = 0

; The sound card's LINE OUT, under the video socket. PC99 colour-codes this lime
; green and the collar is baked that colour, so nothing here sets it — see
; Tools/gen/gen_trs.gd. y 0.294: the VGA flange's bottom edge is at 0.3117 and this
; jack is 10.4 mm across, so the two clear each other by 12 mm.
;
; z -0.2235: the zone sits where a seated plug's ORIGIN belongs, 2 mm proud of the
; panel at -0.2215. Every connector has its own standoff — the phono trio use 10 mm
; and the VGA socket 7.5 mm — so this is not a number to copy from the neighbours.
;
; direction = 0 is OUT. `channel` is not set: TrsPort pins it to AUDIO_STEREO, which
; is what one stereo connector carries and what no phono socket can express.
;
; The tower keeps its three phono sockets. Sound can still leave that way to a
; television; this is the path to a pair of powered speakers.
[node name="LineOut" parent="Back" instance=ExtResource("3_trsport")]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, -1, -0.055, 0.294, -0.2235)
direction = 0
